(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-02"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Physics is a quantitative science: it measures quantities such as length, mass and time. A measured quantity is written as a number followed by a unit, for example 5 m. Because different quantities have different units, a standard system of units, the International System (SI), is used so that everyone can compare results. This chapter covers units, measurement, dimensions, errors and significant figures.","x":["Physics is a science of measurement. To measure a quantity, we compare it with a standard of the same kind, called a unit. A measurement is written as a number and a unit, such as 5 m or 20 s. A number without a unit has no meaning in physics.","Different quantities have different units, and in the past, different countries used different systems. This made it hard to compare results. So the scientists agreed on one international system, the SI, which is used in science all over the world, and in India as well.","The aim of this chapter is to learn the SI units, to see how lengths, masses and times are measured, to use dimensions to check equations, and to understand errors and significant figures. These ideas help you in every practical and every numerical problem of physics.","Study tip: always write the unit with each number during a calculation, and convert all the data to the same system before you start."],"e":"A student writes the length of a table as 2. The reader cannot tell if it is 2 m, 2 cm or 2 feet. The correct way is to write the number with its unit, for example 2 m."},"1.2":{"s":"Earlier systems of units were CGS, MKS and FPS. Since 1971 the SI system has been used all over the world. It has seven fundamental quantities with their units: length (metre), mass (kilogram), time (second), temperature (kelvin), electric current (ampere), luminous intensity (candela) and amount of substance (mole). All other quantities, such as velocity, are derived quantities and their units are derived from these seven.","x":["Earlier systems of units were CGS (centimetre, gram, second), MKS (metre, kilogram, second) and FPS (foot, pound, second). Since 1971, the SI system (Système International) has been in use all over the world. It is a coherent system, which means that the derived units follow from the base units without extra factors.","The SI has seven base quantities with their units: length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol) and luminous intensity (candela, cd). Every other quantity is derived from these. For example, the unit of speed is m/s, and the unit of force, the newton, is kg m s⁻².","Prefixes make the units larger or smaller by powers of ten: kilo (10³), mega (10⁶), giga (10⁹), milli (10⁻³), micro (10⁻⁶), nano (10⁻⁹) and pico (10⁻¹²). Two supplementary units are the radian for plane angles and the steradian for solid angles.","Common mistake: thinking that the gram is the SI unit of mass. It is the kilogram. Another mistake: writing the kelvin with a degree sign. The unit is K, not °K."],"e":"Convert 72 km/h into SI units. 72 × 1000 m / 3600 s = 20 m/s. A shortcut: to change km/h into m/s, divide by 3.6."},"1.3":{"s":"Length is measured with a metre scale for everyday objects, and by other methods for very large or very small distances. For large distances like the distance to a planet or star we use the parallax method: the same object is seen from two points a known distance apart and the small angle is measured. The size of a planet is found from the angle it subtends and its distance. Very small sizes are measured with an electron microscope.","f":[{"x":"D = b/theta","n":"Parallax method: D = distance of the object, b = distance between the two observing points, theta = parallax angle in radian (small).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.3, p.4"},{"x":"alpha = d/D","n":"Angular size of a planet: d = diameter of the planet, D = its distance, alpha in radian.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.3, p.4"}],"w":["Put the angle in RADIANS before using D = b/θ; 1 arcsec = 4.85 × 10⁻⁶ rad."],"x":["A metre scale measures everyday lengths. Smaller lengths use a vernier calliper or a screw gauge, and larger lengths need indirect methods. For very large distances, like that to the Moon, a planet or a star, we use the parallax method.","Parallax is the apparent shift of an object when it is seen from two different places. Look at a planet from two points that are a distance b apart, called the baseline, and measure the angle θ between the two lines of sight. If the planet is far away, then the distance is D = b/θ, where θ is in radians. For the angle, 1 arcsec = 4.85 × 10⁻⁶ rad. To find the size of the planet, measure the small angle α that it subtends, and then the diameter is d = αD.","The vernier calliper has a least count of 0.1 mm (or 0.01 cm), and the screw gauge of 0.01 mm. The least count of a vernier is the difference between one main scale division and one vernier scale division. Very small lengths, like the size of a molecule, are found by other methods, such as electron microscopes.","Common mistake: using the angle in degrees in D = b/θ. The angle must be in radians, and the baseline must be small compared with the distance."],"e":"Baseline b = 3.0 × 10¹¹ m, and parallax angle 2 arcsec = 9.7 × 10⁻⁶ rad. D = b/θ = 3.0 × 10¹¹/9.7 × 10⁻⁶ = 3.1 × 10¹⁶ m, which is about 3.3 light years."},"1.4":{"s":"The SI unit of mass is the kilogram. Until 2019 it was the mass of a platinum-iridium cylinder kept in France; it is now defined through a fixed value of Planck's constant. For atoms and molecules, where the kilogram is inconvenient, the atomic mass unit is used: one unified atomic mass unit is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.","x":["The SI unit of mass is the kilogram (kg). Before 2019, it was defined as the mass of a particular cylinder of platinum and iridium that is kept near Paris. A physical object can change a little with time, so since 2019, the kilogram is defined by fixing the value of Planck's constant.","For very small masses, like those of atoms and molecules, the kilogram is not convenient. So we use the unified atomic mass unit (u). One u is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom, and 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. The mass of a proton is about 1.007 u, and the mass of an electron is about 0.00055 u.","Large masses, like those of planets, are found indirectly, from their gravitational effect on other bodies, using the law of gravitation. Common masses: an electron 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, a proton 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, the Earth 6 × 10²⁴ kg, and the Sun 2 × 10³⁰ kg.","Common mistake: confusing mass and weight. Mass, in kg, is the amount of matter and does not change from place to place. Weight is a force, in newton, and it changes with the value of g."],"e":"A carbon-12 atom has a mass of exactly 12 u. So its mass in kg is 12 × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁶ kg."},"1.5":{"s":"The SI unit of time is the second. It was once defined from the mean solar day, but the length of a day changes slowly, so it is now defined by an atomic clock based on the radiation of the caesium atom. Time is also measured by clocks based on other periodic events.","x":["The SI unit of time is the second (s). In the past, it was defined from the mean solar day, which is the time that the Earth takes to rotate once, divided into 24 × 60 × 60 parts. But the Earth does not turn at an exactly steady rate, and the length of a day changes slowly.","So the second is now defined with an atomic clock. One second is the time for a fixed, very large number (9 192 631 770) of cycles of the radiation that the caesium-133 atom gives out in a particular transition. Atomic clocks are accurate to about one second in millions of years.","Other clocks use other periodic events. A pendulum clock uses the swings of a pendulum, a quartz watch uses the vibrations of a quartz crystal, and a stopwatch is used in the laboratory for short times. A time interval is measured from its start to its end, and the least count of the clock decides how exact it is.","Useful figures: the age of the universe is about 4 × 10¹⁷ s. A day is 86 400 s. A year is about 3.15 × 10⁷ s."],"e":"A stopwatch shows 12.4 s, 12.5 s and 12.3 s for the same event. The mean is 12.4 s. The least count of the watch is 0.1 s, so the reading is given to one place after the decimal."},"1.6":{"s":"The dimensions of a physical quantity show how it depends on the fundamental quantities: mass M, length L, time T, current I, temperature K. Velocity has dimensions [L¹T⁻¹]. Dimensional analysis can check whether an equation is correct, convert units and suggest relations. It cannot give dimensionless constants or handle trigonometric, exponential or logarithmic functions, and it cannot tell if a quantity is a sum of terms.","f":[{"x":"[velocity] = [L T^-1], [acceleration] = [L T^-2], [force] = [M L T^-2]","n":"Dimensions of some common quantities. Energy and work are [M L^2 T^-2], and pressure is [M L^-1 T^-2].","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.6, p.6"}],"w":["An equation can be dimensionally right and still wrong (a missing number such as 1/2 is not caught).","Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted."],"x":["The dimensions of a physical quantity show how it depends on the base quantities. The base quantities are mass [M], length [L], time [T], current [I], temperature [K] and so on. The dimensional formula is written with the powers of these in square brackets. Velocity is [L T⁻¹], acceleration is [L T⁻²], force is [M L T⁻²], work and energy are [M L² T⁻²], and pressure is [M L⁻¹ T⁻²].","The principle of homogeneity: in a correct equation, all the terms have the same dimensions, and only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted. Uses of dimensional analysis: (1) to check whether an equation can be right, (2) to change units from one system to another, (3) to suggest a relation between quantities, when we know what it depends on.","How to use it to find a relation: write T = k lˣ gʸ, put in the dimensions of each quantity, and equate the powers of M, L and T on both sides. This gives T proportional to √(l/g). The number k can not be found by this method.","Limits: it cannot give dimensionless constants like 1/2 or 2π. It cannot handle trigonometric, logarithmic or exponential functions. And an equation can be dimensionally right and still wrong, for example s = ut + at², which misses the factor 1/2."],"e":"Check v² = u² + 2as. The dimensions of v² are [L² T⁻²]. The dimensions of 2as are [L T⁻²][L] = [L² T⁻²]. All the terms agree, so the equation can be correct, and it is."},"1.7":{"s":"Accuracy tells how close a measurement is to the true value. Precision tells how close repeated measurements are to each other. A measurement can be precise without being accurate. Uncertainty in an observation comes from the quality of the instrument, the skill of the person, the method used, and the external conditions of the experiment.","x":["Accuracy tells how close a measured value is to the true value. Precision tells how close the repeated measurements are to each other. A measurement can be precise but not accurate. For example, a faulty scale with a zero error can give readings that agree with each other but are all wrong by the same amount.","Uncertainty is present in every measurement. It comes from the instrument, such as its least count, from the person, such as the reaction time, from the method used, and from the conditions around, such as changes in temperature. We can reduce uncertainty but never remove it completely.","The result of a measurement should always be written with its uncertainty, like 20.2 ± 0.1 cm. It tells the reader how far to trust the number. A better instrument, a better method and more readings all give a smaller uncertainty.","Common mistake: using the words accurate and precise as if they mean the same thing. Think of a dart board. Darts that are close together are precise. Darts that are close to the centre are accurate."],"e":"True length: 20.00 cm. Readings: 20.51, 20.52, 20.50, 20.51 cm. They are close to each other (precise), but they are 0.5 cm from the true value (not accurate). A zero error of the scale is likely."},"1.8":{"s":"Errors in measurement are systematic or random. Systematic errors push the result in one direction: instrumental, from the method, or personal. Random errors vary unpredictably and are reduced by averaging many readings. The mean of the readings is the most probable value. The absolute error is the difference from the mean, and the mean absolute error is their average. Relative error is that divided by the mean, and percentage error is relative error × 100.","f":[{"x":"a_mean = (a_1 + a_2 + ... + a_n)/n","n":"Arithmetic mean of n readings: the most probable value.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta a_n = |a_mean - a_n|","n":"Absolute error in the n-th reading.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta a_mean = (Delta a_1 + ... + Delta a_n)/n","n":"Mean absolute error.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Relative error = Delta a_mean/a_mean","n":"Divide by the mean value; the percentage error is this times 100.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta Z = Delta A + Delta B","n":"Maximum absolute error in a sum or a difference Z = A + B or Z = A - B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.10"},{"x":"Delta Z/Z = Delta A/A + Delta B/B","n":"Maximum relative error in a product or a quotient.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.10"},{"x":"Delta Z/Z = n*(Delta A/A)","n":"Maximum relative error in Z = A^n. For Z = A^p B^q/C^r: p*(Delta A/A) + q*(Delta B/B) + r*(Delta C/C).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.11"}],"w":["For both sums AND differences the absolute errors ADD.","For a product, a quotient or a power the RELATIVE errors add, each multiplied by its power."],"x":["Errors are of two main kinds. Systematic errors push all the readings the same way. Examples: an instrumental error like a zero error, an error of the method, and a personal error such as always reading from the wrong angle. They can be corrected once they are found. Random errors vary from one reading to another without a pattern. Taking many readings and finding the mean reduces them.","For readings a₁, a₂, …, aₙ: the mean is a_mean = (a₁ + … + aₙ)/n, and it is the most probable value. The absolute error of a reading is Δaᵢ = |a_mean − aᵢ|. The mean absolute error is the average of these. The relative error is the mean absolute error divided by the mean, and the percentage error is the relative error × 100.","How errors combine: for a sum or a difference, Z = A ± B, the absolute errors add: ΔZ = ΔA + ΔB. For a product or a quotient, Z = A × B or A/B, the relative errors add: ΔZ/Z = ΔA/A + ΔB/B. For a power, Z = Aⁿ, ΔZ/Z = n(ΔA/A).","Common mistake: subtracting errors for a difference. The errors never cancel in the worst case, so they add. And for a quantity with a power, such as r³, the relative error is multiplied by the power."],"e":"Radius of a sphere r = 2.50 ± 0.01 cm. The relative error in r is 0.01/2.50 = 0.4 percent. The volume depends on r³, so its relative error is 3 × 0.4 = 1.2 percent."},"1.9":{"s":"Significant figures are the digits in a measurement that are known reliably, plus one doubtful digit. Their number tells how accurate the measurement is. A reading of 15.43 cm from a scale with a least count of 0.1 cm has the last digit uncertain. Using an instrument with a smaller least count gives more significant figures. In a result, you should not report more figures than the data justify.","w":["The least count of the instrument decides how many figures are significant: a ruler marked in mm gives a result to 0.1 cm."],"x":["Significant figures are the digits of a measured value that are known reliably, plus one doubtful digit. The number of significant figures shows how good the measurement is. A reading of 15.4 cm from a scale with a least count of 0.1 cm has three significant figures, and the last one is doubtful.","Rules for counting: all non-zero digits count. Zeros between non-zero digits count. Zeros at the start of a number do not count, as they only show the place of the decimal point (0.00450 has three). Zeros at the end count if there is a decimal point (4.50 has three). Writing in scientific notation helps: 4.50 × 10⁻³ has three.","Rules for calculations: in addition and subtraction, the result has as many decimal places as the number with the fewest decimal places. In multiplication and division, the result has as many significant figures as the number with the fewest significant figures. Round only at the end of the calculation, and not in the middle.","A smaller least count of an instrument gives more significant figures. Common mistake: giving all the digits of a calculator. If the data has 2 significant figures, the answer should not have 8."],"e":"The sides of a rectangle are 4.25 cm (3 significant figures) and 2.1 cm (2 figures). The product is 8.925 cm², which is written as 8.9 cm² with 2 significant figures."}};
